Package: Pando
Version: 1.5.1
Type: Package
Title: Inferring regulomes from multi-modal single-cell genomics data
Authors@R: person(given = "Jonas Simon",
               family = "Fleck",
               role = c("aut", "cre"),
               email = "jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch",
               comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-4686-7254"))
Maintainer: Jonas Simon Fleck <jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch>
Description: Pando jointly uses scRNA-seq and scATAC-seq measurements to infer
    regulatory relationships between TF-binding site pairs and target genes.
    infer_condition_grn automatically uses the original infer_grn workflow when
    no condition column is supplied, the requested column is absent, or fewer
    than two condition levels are observed; that route creates no condition
    coefficients. With two or more conditions, one exact TF-peak-target
    candidate supergraph and an equal-condition within-condition variance scale
    are used within each broad cell type. The condition engine uses nested
    condition-stratified cell cross-fitting, sparse-group shared-support
    selection, and a support-constrained hierarchical-ridge refit. The numerical
    core keeps TF-by-ATAC designs sparse, reads canonical dgCMatrix slots without
    S4-to-dense coercion, initializes FISTA from centered spectral estimates and
    uses only the compiled Eigen proximal-gradient path solver. The TF-by-ATAC
    product kernel is also C++-only; missing registration, unsupported sparse
    classes and native execution errors terminate the analysis. Bootstrap
    stability, ridge-grid sensitivity and related sensitivity refits are
    excluded from the canonical workflow. The primary projection is
    condition-full outer-heldout support: jointly estimable edges form the common
    component, condition-specific estimable edges remain in the focal condition,
    and every non-estimable edge contributes a projectable structural zero in
    that condition. Exact-zero predictors remain represented in the shared
    candidate supergraph without being assigned fitted coefficients.
